Parse, verify, and display the contents of a BuildWarden ledger.

The path can be a ledger file directly, or a warden output directory
(the ledger will be found automatically, compressed or not).

Verifies the cryptographic signature chain and displays a summary of all
recorded network requests and artifacts. Exits 0 if the ledger is valid,
exits 1 if verification fails.

The build environment record is decoded and its payload (environment.json
beside the ledger) checked against the signed record. With --verify-disks,
each VM disk layer it names is re-hashed at its recorded path, which reads
every byte of every base image.

Usage:
  warden inspect <path> [flags]

Examples:
  warden inspect warden-output
  warden inspect warden-output/ledger.zst
  warden inspect --json warden-output
  warden inspect -v ledger.bin
  warden inspect -vv ledger.bin
  warden inspect --verify-disks warden-output

Flags:
      --extract string   extract captured payloads to directory
  -h, --help             help for inspect
      --json             output as JSON
      --no-color         disable colored output
  -v, --verbose count    increase verbosity (-v=tree, -vv=full)
      --verify-disks     re-hash the VM disk layers the environment record names

Global Flags:
      --color string     color output (auto, always, never)
      --config string    config file to read, the only one that is (default: WARDEN_CONFIG, then ./warden.toml if it exists)
      --driver string    orchestration driver (container, qemu, vz, hyperv, firecracker)
      --runtime string   container runtime (finch, docker, podman)
